<h2>Applying It — Context vs Connections, Finding Your Level</h2>
<p>Once you have settled on a level, here comes the real practical question. <strong>"Should everything go into the Second Brain?"</strong> The answer is <strong>no</strong>. You need a criterion that separates what to <strong>ingest</strong> from what to <strong>leave out and only reach into</strong>.</p>

<h3>Of the 4C, the Second Brain is the first two</h3>
<p>Break an AI operating system into <strong>4C</strong> and it looks like this. In a Second Brain what matters is mostly <strong>the first two</strong>.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>4C</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Second Brain?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Context</strong></td><td>What my business has done — quarterly goals, decisions, history</td><td>✅ The core target</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Connections</strong></td><td>Live data that changes constantly — Slack, email, customer data</td><td>△ Don't ingest, just reach in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>What tools and skills let you do</td><td>— A separate area</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cadence</td><td>When and how often it runs</td><td>— A separate area</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h3>The test — "Will it still be useful a year from now?"</h3>
<p>When you are unsure whether to <strong>ingest</strong> a piece of data, filter it with this one sentence.</p>

<div class="diagram">
<svg viewBox="0 0 660 180" role="img" aria-label="The test: if it is still useful a year later, ingest it (evergreen); what changes by next week is not ingested, only reached into">
    <polygon class="d-box accent" points="330,20 400,60 330,100 260,60"/>
    <text class="d-label sm" x="330" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Still useful</text>
    <text class="d-label sm" x="330" y="72" text-anchor="middle">a year later?</text>

    <rect class="d-box" x="60" y="120" width="220" height="48" rx="10"/>
    <text class="d-label sm" x="170" y="140" text-anchor="middle">Yes → ingest it (Context)</text>
    <text class="d-sub" x="170" y="156" text-anchor="middle">Evergreen: goals · decisions · history</text>

    <rect class="d-box soft" x="380" y="120" width="220" height="48" rx="10"/>
    <text class="d-label sm" x="490" y="140" text-anchor="middle">No → don't ingest (Connections)</text>
    <text class="d-sub" x="490" y="156" text-anchor="middle">Volatile: Slack · email → access only</text>

    <path class="d-edge accent" d="M300 78 C240 100, 200 104, 175 118"/><polygon class="d-arrow accent" points="169.9,110 175.9,120 180.9,111"/>
    <path class="d-edge" d="M360 78 C420 100, 460 104, 485 118"/><polygon class="d-arrow" points="478.9,111 484.9,120 489.9,110"/>
</svg>
<div class="d-cap">If it is not evergreen it becomes noise, and you have to delete the old material every month. Volatile data should be <strong>left out and looked at on demand</strong>.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Examples — what to ingest vs what to leave</span>
    <strong>Ingest (Context):</strong> "this quarter's goals (OTAA) and their status", "the decisions we made and why". <br>
    <strong>Leave (Connections):</strong> last week's Slack conversation with John, a specific customer's live data — for these it is enough that the Second Brain <strong>can go and look</strong>.
</div>

<h3>How to answer a vague question — sequential fallback lookup</h3>
<p>The real power of a Second Brain is that even for a <strong>vague question</strong> it knows <strong>where to look and in what order</strong>. For example: <em>"That goal number 7 thing I talked about with John last week — what was it?"</em></p>

<div class="diagram">
